<div class="blurb">
<div class="header"><h2>what is msg/dsg</h2></div>
<div class="body">
<p>Monosodium glutamate (MSG) was first prepared in <span class="highlight">1908</span> by Japanese biochemist Kikunae Ikeda, with commercial production beginning in <span class="highlight">1909</span> <sup><a href="#src-msg-history">msg-history</a>, <a href="#src-msg-history-2">msg-history-2</a>, <a href="#src-msg-history-3">msg-history-3</a>, <a href="#src-msg-history-4">msg-history-4</a>, <a href="#src-msg-history-5">msg-history-5</a></sup>. It is the sodium salt of glutamic acid, an amino acid. <span class="highlight">Glutamate occurs naturally in many foods</span> (tomatoes, cheese, mushrooms), but <span class="highlight">MSG is manufactured free glutamic acid</span> that is <span class="highlight">added to food</span> <sup><a href="#src-natural-msg">natural-msg</a>, <a href="#src-natural-msg-2">natural-msg-2</a>, <a href="#src-natural-msg-3">natural-msg-3</a>, <a href="#src-natural-msg-4">natural-msg-4</a></sup>.</p>
<p><em>Note:</em> Strictly speaking, MSG is not the same as L-Glutamate (the naturally occurring amino acid). MSG is the <strong>sodium salt</strong> of L-glutamic acid (L-glutamate + sodium). Only the L-enantiomer produces the umami taste; the D-form has no flavor.</p>
<p>Disodium glutamate (DSG) and related nucleotide enhancers (Disodium Guanylate, E627) were developed in the <span class="highlight">1960s-1970s</span> for synergistic use with MSG <sup><a href="#src-dsg-history">dsg-history</a>, <a href="#src-dsg-history-2">dsg-history-2</a>, <a href="#src-dsg-history-3">dsg-history-3</a>, <a href="#src-dsg-history-4">dsg-history-4</a></sup>. Both MSG and DSG break down into glutamate (glutamic acid) in the body.</p>
<p>Chemical formula: C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>8</sub>NO<sub>4</sub>Na (MSG), C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>7</sub>NO<sub>4</sub>Na<sub>2</sub> (DSG)</p>
</div>
